+Randomized Data Decomposition is a data distribution method where blocks are randomly assigned to processors with equal probability, relying on statistical theorems to guarantee load balance with high probability [S1]. It is primarily used in parallel and out-of-core isocontouring of massive datasets where static load estimation is difficult or expensive [S1]. Unlike its parent [Round-Robin Data Distribution](2667-round-robin-data-distribution.md) and siblings [Static Round-Robin Partitioning](2728-static-round-robin-partitioning.md) and [Scattered Assignment](4204-scattered-assignment.md), it explicitly models workload variance via normalized Bernoulli trials to bound load imbalance probabilistically rather than deterministically [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Inputs:** $N$ data blocks ($b_1, \dots, b_N$) with $N \gg P$; $P$ processors ($p_1, \dots, p_P$); workload $w_i$ for each block $b_i$ at a given isovalue $\nu$; maximum block workload $\hat{w} = \max_{i=1}^N w_i$; total workload $W = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i$.
-- **Outputs:** Assignment map specifying the destination processor for each block; distributed data blocks on their assigned processors.
-- **Data Structures:** Not given in the sources.
